      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "cd78d5c9-cbad-4731-bad7-749a78d3595d",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Remember our pal GitHub Projects? We’re mostly just going to keep doing sprints all term! So pretty much the same.",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "64dc044b-ab07-475a-a6c4-e0a306fd5ee7",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The biggest reason that we’re using GitHub Projects is because it is right there in GitHub already and we’re using it for Pull Requests and version tracking anyway. The reason we don’t use some other work/issue management system such as Asana, etc, is mostly because they are more complicated and don’t integrate with GitHub as nicely. However, they all share the most important core set of features. If you are worried about industry standards, the way we are using GitHub Projects is pretty similar to how people use Asana/Jira/Basecamp etc. The backlog-icebox-inprogress-review-done task workflow is  standard.",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
